  "Question": "In a bag of marbles, \\(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\) of the marbles are blue and the rest are red. If the number of red marbles is doubled and the number of blue marbles stays the same, what fraction of the marbles will be red?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{7}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Measure the marbles in shares equal to one fifth of the original total. Originally, blue marbles occupy \\(3\\) shares and red marbles occupy the remaining \\(2\\) shares. Doubling the red amount gives \\(4\\) shares of red marbles, while blue still occupies \\(3\\) shares.\n\nThe new total is \\(4+3=7\\) shares of the same size. Therefore the fraction that is red is\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{7},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
